# Lines of course-install.sh that NO test on this platform can execute, one per entry, each with
# the text that must be on that line and the reason it cannot run.
#
# Format: LINE  ANCHOR -- reason
#
#   LINE    the line number in course-install.sh -- the installer PROPER, since #221. The
#           bootstrap (install-cs193v.sh) is scored by nothing: it is ~150 lines of download,
#           and 95-installer-coverage.sh says so where it sets INST. The gate checks the line
#           is an EXECUTABLE line --
#           non-blank and not a comment -- because the denominator excludes the others, so an
#           entry that drifts onto one excuses nothing while still reading as an excuse.
#   ANCHOR  literal text that must appear ON that line, checked with grep -F. This is what makes
#           renumbering verifiable, and it exists because it had to: seven of the eight entries
#           this file used to hold had gone stale, three of them onto blank lines, and nothing
#           noticed for as long as the numbers stayed inside the file.
#   reason  why no test on this platform can execute it. The reason is not decoration -- it is
#           the thing a reader checks when they wonder whether an entry still earns its place.
#
# TWO ENTRIES, AND IT USED TO CLAIM EIGHT. The six `podman machine` lines that were here are
# gone because the gate's own hit list says they RUN: podman-fake answers machine init, set,
# start and stop, and 25-installer.sh drives every one of them through a pty. They were excusing
# lines that no longer needed excusing -- which costs nothing directly and is exactly how an
# allowlist stops being read. Checked against hit.lines rather than reasoned about.
#
# So what is left is the pair that no fake can stand in for.
378  msg err.curl-missing-mac -- no tier can reach it. The shim tier PREPENDS to PATH so it cannot hide a real /usr/bin/curl, and the machine that CAN have curl removed is Linux, where this arm is not taken. The anchor is the CALL now rather than the sentence: since issue #116 the words are in the catalogue, which the denominator no longer counts, so the executable line here is the die that fetches them
591  sudo installer -pkg -- needs a Mac; the pkg install itself, which no fake stands in for
#
# RENUMBERED FIVE TIMES DURING #221 -- 655/858 when the logic moved into course-install.sh, then
# twice more as survey's comments grew and shrank, and finally to 378/591 when txt() and the
# 506-line catalogue left the file. The first entry's ANCHOR TEXT changed too, from
# `txt err.curl-missing-mac` to `msg`, when the installer stopped carrying its own accessor.
#
# EVERY ONE OF THOSE WAS CAUGHT BY THE ANCHOR, which is the point of the format: each entry
# names the text that must be ON its line, so a number that lands somewhere else FAILS rather
# than quietly excusing the wrong statement. Not a hypothetical -- it is how all five were
# found, each time in a run whose only other red was nothing at all.
#
# AND FIVE TIMES IS AN ARGUMENT FOR DERIVING THE NUMBER FROM THE ANCHOR rather than checking it
# against one. The anchor already identifies the statement uniquely; the number is a cache of
# where it was, and a cache nobody can update automatically. Left as a follow-up rather than
# done here, because it changes what the gate reads.
#
# curl's retry loop is NOT here: it is not reachable from a file:// tarball and
# would need an HTTP server in the fixture, so it is RECORDED as uncovered by the gate rather
# than excused. An allowlist entry would say "cannot be tested"; the honest statement is
# "not tested yet, and here is what it would take".
